-- DICT-SEMANTICS §8 I1 under the WILDCARD import spelling (RUN-XMOD-054, #1425 F1 repair).
--
-- WHAT THIS ADDS OVER ITS TWO SIBLINGS. `s8-i1-samename-independent-dict-arity/` grades the
-- EMIT path (mangled, accidentally identity-bearing) and a monomorphic value;
-- `s8-i1-samename-unconstrained-poly-callee/` grades an eval-path occurrence reached through
-- an ALIASED import and a same-module local call. Neither contains a wildcard import, and
-- neither does any other fixture in this tree: at the time this was written,
--
--     grep -rn "^import [a-z_]*\.\*" compiler/ stdlib/   ->  zero files
--
-- so the self-compile fixpoint, `selfproc` and `check-self` are all structurally incapable of
-- observing the wildcard-import class. That blind spot is why the #1425 dict-arity fix could
-- repair the aliased arm and BREAK this one in the same motion, with every gate green.
--
-- THE MECHANISM IT GUARDS. A wildcard-imported name contributed NO row to
-- `currentImportDefinersRef`, so `qualConstraintKey` could not resolve it to a definer and
-- `declaredConstraintFor` fell through to the bare-name table -- where `cmod.pick`'s
-- constrained row answered for `umod.pick`. While the bare table was ALSO wrong on the define
-- side the two errors cancelled and this spelling looked correct; once `selfDeclArities` fixed
-- the define by identity, the call site began applying a dict its callee no longer abstracts.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from the declared signatures, NOT captured from any engine):
-- dict arity is keyed by binding identity, so `cmod.pick : Sz a => a -> Int` abstracts one
-- dict and `umod.pick : a -> a` abstracts NONE. `C.pick (1 : Int)` = `sz 1 + 100` = 101.
-- `pick 1 + 200` = `1 + 200` = 201. So: 101 then 201.
--
-- 🚨 UNLIKE ITS `-poly-callee` SIBLING, THE IMPORT ORDER HERE IS **NOT** LOAD-BEARING, AND
-- THAT IS ITSELF THE FINDING. Measured at the pre-repair head `fb7f3544`, BOTH orders:
--
--     import umod.* ; import cmod as C  ->  run exit 1: 101, then
--                                           `E-NOT-A-FUNCTION: applied non-function: <dict:>`
--     import cmod as C ; import umod.*  ->  run exit 1: identical
--
-- Because the wrongness is order-INVARIANT, `test/diff_compiler_import_order.sh` -- whose
-- criterion is invariance across orderings, with no ground truth -- reports this shape as
-- "invariant" and PASSES on the broken compiler. A permutation differential can only see the
-- order-dependent face of this defect family; the value assertion below is what sees this one.
-- That is why this guard lives in `dict_semantics` and not in `import_order`.
--
-- `medaka check` exits 0 and the BUILT BINARY prints the correct 101/201 on the broken
-- compiler too; `run` is the only verb that shows it.
import umod.*
import cmod as C

main =
  println (C.pick (1 : Int))
  println (pick 1 + 200)
